 1==>List Interface : 
 ------------------------
 List extends collection interface.
 List represents the ordered collection of elements.
 List allows duplicates.
 List supports index-based operations.

Important methods of list : 
	-get(index)
	-set(index,element)
	-add(index,element)
	-remove(index)
	-indexOf(element)
	
Implementataion classes of list : 
	-ArrayList,LinkedList,vector->stack.
	
	Method	Meaning	Example
add()	Add element	list.add("Java");
add(index, element)	Add at particular position	list.add(1, "Python");
get(index)	Get element	list.get(0);
set(index, element)	Replace element	list.set(0, "C++");
remove(index)	Remove by index	list.remove(0);
remove(object)	Remove particular element	list.remove("Java");
contains()	Check element exists	list.contains("Java");
size()	Number of elements	list.size();
isEmpty()	Check whether empty	list.isEmpty();
clear()	Remove all elements	list.clear();
indexOf()	First occurrence index	list.indexOf("Java");
lastIndexOf()	Last occurrence index	list.lastIndexOf("Java");
	
1.ArrayList :
	-ArrayList implements the List Interface.
	-It internally uses resizeable array.
	-It maintains insertion order.
	-It allows dupicates.
	-It has fast random access using index.
	(It internally implements Random access interface)
	-Time complexity is o(1)
	-Insertion and removal in the middle may require shifting of the elements 
	-ArrayList is not sychronized.
	-It is best suited for the search operations.
	 
2.LinkedList :
	-LinkedList implements List interface.
	-It internally works different from ArrayList.
	-It allows null
	-It maintains insertion order.
	-It allows nodes rather than a resizable array.
	-It also implements Deque interface.
	
3.Stack :

	- Stack is a class in java.util package.
	- It reperesents a LIFO(last In First Out) data structure 
	- The last element inserted in the first element removed.

		eg: Stack of books on a table
    	Stack of plates in a table

	Stacks extends Vector
	So, Stack indirectly implements the List interface 
	Since, stack extends vector, its inherited methods are synchronized
	
	Methods :
	1.push() -add element to the top 
	2.pop() -removes and returns the top element
	3.peek() -returns the top element without removing 
	
	Stack allows the duplicates .
	It maintains the insertion order.
	
	Cursors : 
	=========
	
	A cursor is an object used to traverse or access elements of a collection one by one.
	
	In java collections framework, we have 3 main cursors : 
	1.Enumeration : 
		-Oldest type of cursor in java .
		-It is mainly used with vector and hashTable 
		-Enumeration can retrieve elements but it cannot remove them.
		
		Methods : 
		hasMoreElement()
		hasNextElement()
		
	2.iterator :
	 widely used
	 universal cursor used in any type of collection.
	 -Iterator is a cursor used to traverse elements of collection one by one in forward direction.(uni directional)
	 -Iterator is interface it supports works with most collections implementation like : LinkedList,ArrayList,Vector,Stack,HashSet,LinkedHashSet,TreeSet.
	 -It can read and remove elements while traversing.
	 -It does not provide add() and set() methods.
	 -It is unidirectional ,traverse only in forward direction.
	 
	 Methods:
	 
	 -hasNext()
	 -next()
	 -remove()
	
	3.Listiterator : 
	Listiterator extends iterator interface .
	It is avaiable only for List implementations.
	Examples: ArrayList,LinkedList,Vector and stack.
	It supports bidirectional traversal - forward and backward .
	It can read ,add , remove and replace elements during iteration.
	
	
3.Set :

set is an interface in java.util package.
Its implementations are : hashset,linkedhashset and treeset.

	1.HashSet
		-hashset do not allow the duplicates.
		-hashset will not maintain the insertion order.
		
	*For classes like String, java already provides proper equals() and hashCode(). so if duplicates Strings or integers are added into the hashSet ,hashset can detect that they are duplicated.
	*For custom class like student ,
	Even though two objects are having similar data,hashset considers them as two different objects because the equals() method in hashset will check only the object identity(address).
		
	*We need to override equals() and hashcode() to avoid dupication of custom objects.
	
	*For this , first we need to find the logical definations of equality
	for example : 
	for student objects,
	we can say ,that the two students are same if their studentid is same 
	OR
	for Employee object :
	we can say that two employees are same if their employeeid is same. 
	
	
	2.LinkedHashSet : 
		-LinkedHashSet will maintain the insertion order.
		-It will not allow the duplicates.
	
	COMPARABLE INTERFACE : 
	
	comparable is an interface,it is used to define natural or default ordering of objects of a class.
	for eg: if we have many employee objects,java does not automatically know whether employees should be sorted by id or name or something else.
	By implementing comparable interface,
	we can tell java how to sort the employee objects by overriding the method compareTo.
	compareable interface is available in java.lang package 
	It is generic interface.
	The class whose objects need to be compared implements comparable interface.
	we override the compareTo method.
	After override the compareTo method.
	compareTO() method : 
	
	syntax : 
	object1.compareTo(object2)
	
	eg: emp1.compareTo(emp2)
	
	compareTo() will returns an integer value.
	It returns 
	 - 0 -> both objects are equal .
	 - negative value -> first object is lesser than the second object value.It returns the current object must come before the other object.
	 - It returns a positive value when the current object must come after the other object.
	 
	 COMPARATOR : 
	 
	 Comparator is an interface.
	 It is uses to define custom custom sorting logic for objects .
	 It is present in java.util.
	 
	 with comparable, we can define only one natural ordering inside the table.
	 with comparator, we can define multiple different sorting rules outside the class
It has a method:
	 int compare(T obj1,T obj2 );
	 It compares two objects at a time.
	 
	 compare () method returns an int value : 
	 	 - 0 -> both objects are equal .
	 - negative value -> first object is lesser than the second object value.It returns the current object must come before the other object.
	 - It returns a positive value when the current object must come after the other object.
	 
	Comparable vs comparator
	===============================
	1.comparable is in java.lang
	comparator is in java.util
	2.comparable must be used in the same class
	comparator is used in an external different class which is used as custom comparator.
	3.comparable interface has a single method 
	int compareTo(T o)
	comparator interface has a method : 
	int compare(T o1 , T o2)
	4.comparable defines natural/default orderring of a class 
	comparator defines custom ordering.
 	we use comparable when there is only way to sort the objects.
 	we use comparator when we need different ways to sort the same objects.

 
 